Exact corner bounds for signed moments, 11 October 2026

Use q=1-p, v=pq, 0<p<1/2, s>=0, and 0<=alpha<=1. With u=2alpha-1,
beta=[v(1-2p)+su]/(v+s). It increases in alpha. For fixed p,alpha it is
fractional linear in s with positive denominator, hence monotone or constant.
Its p derivative has numerator (1-2p)s[(1-2p)-u]-2v(v+s).
On the rectangular box p in[l,h],s in[S,T],alpha in[A,B], its numerator
is at most (1-2l)T*max(0,1-2l-(2A-1))-2l(1-l)[l(1-l)+S].
If this upper bound is nonpositive, beta decreases in p throughout the box;
its extrema are therefore among the eight literal rational corners. Equality
and s=0 are included. If the gate fails, retain the old interval enclosure.

For s>0, solve the same identity for alpha:
alpha=(beta+1)/2+v(beta-1+2p)/(2s).
It increases in beta, and is fractional linear in s. Its p derivative has
numerator beta-1+(6-2beta)p-6p^2. This numerator increases in beta because
1-2p>0. Since6-2beta>=4, an interval lower bound is
beta_lower-1+(6-2beta_lower)*p_lower-6*p_upper^2.
If nonnegative, alpha increases in p throughout the box, and eight exact
rational corner evaluations enclose every possible alpha. If the gate fails,
use the old interval formula. This contraction retains every actual point of
the original box; no attainment or independence assertion is involved.

Only count-wrapper interval evaluation changes. The stored tree remains a
